<h2>How a 1967–69 Camaro VIN is laid out</h2>
<p>First-generation Camaros carry a 13-character VIN, for example <strong>124377N100001</strong>. Every character has a fixed job:</p>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-silver-300 border border-white/10"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-silver-200"><tr><th class="p-3">Position</th><th class="p-3">Example</th><th class="p-3">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1</td><td class="p-3">1</td><td class="p-3">Chevrolet division</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">2</td><td class="p-3">2</td><td class="p-3">Camaro</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">3</td><td class="p-3">4</td><td class="p-3">Engine type: 3 = 6-cylinder, 4 = V8</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">4–5</td><td class="p-3">37</td><td class="p-3">Body: 37 = sport coupe, 67 = convertible</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">6</td><td class="p-3">7</td><td class="p-3">Model year: 7 = 1967, 8 = 1968, 9 = 1969</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">7</td><td class="p-3">N</td><td class="p-3">Plant: N = Norwood, Ohio; L = Van Nuys (Los Angeles), California</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">8–13</td><td class="p-3">100001</td><td class="p-3">Plant sequence number</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Sequence starting numbers</h2>
<p>Each plant restarted its count every model year. A sequence below the year’s starting number is a red flag worth investigating.</p>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-silver-300 border border-white/10"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-silver-200"><tr><th class="p-3">Model year</th><th class="p-3">First sequence number</th><th class="p-3">Example first Norwood V8 coupe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1967</td><td class="p-3">100001</td><td class="p-3">124377N100001</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1968</td><td class="p-3">300001</td><td class="p-3">124378N300001</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1969</td><td class="p-3">500001</td><td class="p-3">124379N500001</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Norwood built more than 100,000 Camaros a year, so its numbers run past the next “hundred-thousand” mark (for example into 2xxxxx for a 1967). The sequence counts every Camaro at that plant, not one body style or engine.</p>
<h2>What the VIN does not tell you</h2>
<p>A 1967–69 Camaro VIN can’t prove Z/28, SS, RS, Pace Car or COPO status, and it doesn’t name the exact engine. It only tells 6-cylinder from V8. Those details come from the cowl (trim) tag, the engine block’s stamped suffix code and partial VIN, the Protect-O-Plate and other documentation. Be careful with the trim tag: its style number (for example 12437) looks like the VIN prefix, but on the trim tag the third digit isn’t an engine code.</p>
<h2>Where to find it</h2>
<p>On 1967 Camaros the VIN plate is on the driver-side front door hinge pillar, visible with the door open. From 1968 it moved to the top of the dash on the driver’s side, visible through the windshield. Cars built from August 1969 also show the VIN on a sticker on the driver’s door.</p>
<h2>Checking a Camaro before you buy</h2>
<ul><li>Decode the VIN above and confirm the year, plant and V8/6 match what the seller claims.</li><li>Match the plate rivets and location to factory style for that year.</li><li>Compare the partial VIN stamped on the engine pad and transmission with the plate.</li><li>Run a title check through an <a href="https://vehiclehistory.bja.ojp.gov/nmvtis_vehiclehistory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NMVTIS provider</a> and get a marque-savvy inspection.</li></ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can a Camaro VIN tell me if it is a real Z/28 or SS?</h3><p>No. A 1967–69 Camaro VIN only shows 6-cylinder versus V8, body style, year, plant and sequence. Z/28, SS and RS status must be confirmed through the trim tag, engine stamping and documentation.</p><h3>What does N or L mean in a Camaro VIN?</h3><p>The seventh character is the assembly plant: N is Norwood, Ohio and L is Van Nuys (Los Angeles), California.</p><h3>What is the first 1969 Camaro VIN?</h3><p>1969 Camaro sequence numbers started at 500001 at each plant, so the first V8 coupe from Norwood would read 124379N500001.</p>
